  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn area of low pressure, currently centered over Kentucky and \nTennessee, is projected to meander deeper into the southeastern US \nearly this week. Moisture associated with this system, and a nearby \nfront, will continue to feed into the area and facilitate chances of \nshowers and thunderstorms this weekend. \n\nThe best potential for activity should be in the southeast half of \nthe forecast area today, followed by a lull overnight. Moderate to \nheavy rain over already saturated soils could lead to instances of \nlocalized flash flooding; therefore, the Flood Watch remains in \neffect along and south of the I-64 corridor through this evening.\n\nSome additional showers or storms could sprout up on Monday, but \ncoverage should be somewhat limited as drier air edges in behind \nthe receding system.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nDrier and warmer weather is expected Tuesday through midweek, with \ntemperatures likely breaking into the low 90s in the lowlands \nWednesday afternoon. Precipitation chances may return with  \ndisturbances late week into the weekend, though confidence in \nthe timing of these disturbances remains low.",
